First target: Indigo Hammer.

Original functional idea: force unlike things through one fixed comparison/translation anchor so differences and conserved structure become easier to expose.

Re-mark it mathematically:

H_A(X)=\Pi_A(X)

where \Pi_A projects X into a chosen anchor-space A.

For two objects:

\Delta_A(X,Y)=\Pi_A(X)\ominus \Pi_A(Y)

That immediately exposes something hidden: the Hammer is only as good as its anchor-space.

So define:

q_A = \text{anchor quality}

and split it into at least:

q_A =
f(
\text{coverage},
\text{discrimination},
\text{invariance preservation},
\text{distortion}
)

A bad universal nail makes everything look artificially similar. A useful Hammer creates comparability without erasing the distinctions we care about.

So Indigo Hammer gains a diagnostic:

\boxed{
\text{Hammer Strength} \neq \text{Hammer Quality}
}

More forcing can actually reduce information.

That gives:

H(X;A,\lambda)

where \lambda is projection pressure.

Low \lambda: preserve native structure, weak comparability.

High \lambda: strong normalization, higher risk of distortion.

That means the Hammer has the same kind of operating envelope we just discovered in ZPC.

