Now run Kintsugi.

Original:

X_{\text{broken}}
\rightarrow
\text{repair preserving fracture history}

Re-mark as an optimization problem:

X^*
=
\arg\max_Y
\left[
F(Y)
+
\alpha T(Y,X)
+
\beta V(\text{fracture})
\right]

where:

* F = restored function
* T = continuity with original
* V = informational value retained from failure/fracture

This exposes a major distinction:

\boxed{\text{Repair} \neq \text{Restoration}}

Restoration tries to return:

X' \approx X_{\text{before}}

Kintsugi can instead produce:

X' > X_{\text{before}}

because the failure becomes part of the model.

So Kintsugi needs a variable for scar retention:

s \in [0,1]

Too little s: failure information gets erased.

Too much s: the repair becomes dominated by the failure.

Therefore:

K(X;s)

and useful repair depends on:

U_K=
\text{function restored}
+
\text{lesson retained}
-
\text{failure overfitting}

That’s useful for debugging too. A system shouldn’t merely patch bugs; it should selectively preserve the reason the bug was possible.

